  "title": "The trisum law",
  "status": "Proved",
  "claim": "Let T be a connected cubic signed system with a Pfaffian signing that is a trisum of three pieces P1, P2, P3 along a square C = (c0, c1, c2, c3): c0, c2 points and c1, c3 lines shared by the pieces, each with one incidence into each piece, the square's own edges deleted. Give each piece the square's four edges back with a Pfaffian signing (so each piece is a connected cubic system that is not colourable). The leaning split (alpha_i, beta_i) of P_i at C is half the number of colourings of P_i - c1 in which the third point of c3 agrees, as seen by c3, with c0, respectively with c2 (exactly one of the two holds); it does not depend on the Pfaffian signing chosen. (1) Support lemma: inside T every piece is in exactly one of two cases at C for each colouring of c0 and c2, so its boundary behaviour is carried by two numbers. (2) Seen from c3 (colourings of P_i - c3, leaning of the third point of c1) the split is (beta_i, alpha_i), and reversing the piece's orientation (exchanging c0 and c2) swaps alpha_i and beta_i. (3) The trisum law: A(T, L) = rho1 rho2 rho3 - alpha1 alpha2 alpha3 - beta1 beta2 beta3 for every line L, where rho_i = alpha_i + beta_i is the amplitude of P_i: the three pieces' leanings are not all equal. (4) Children as weights: if a piece P of a larger system is glued at a square D to two pieces Q and R, the count of the larger system is a sum over P's colourings in which D's two lines are both proper, weighted on each of P's leanings at D by rho_Q rho_R - alpha_Q alpha_R or rho_Q rho_R - beta_Q beta_R, with the two roles exchanged between D's two lines.",
